 "result": "This repo is a Flask-like framework called `tessel`, so the names differ from Flask's.\n\n**How `request`, `session` and `g` get into templates**\n- **Environment globals:** `Tessel.create_jinja_environment()` in `src/tessel/app.py:467` sets `request`, `session`, `g` and `config` as Jinja globals (lines 493–503). That is why imported templates can see them too.\n- **Default context processor:** `_base_template_vars()` in `src/tessel/templating.py:21` is registered by default in `template_ctx_injectors` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:184`). It swaps `g` and `request` for the concrete objects, which is faster than the proxies. It leaves `session` as the proxy so access tracking still works.\n- **Per-render step:** `render_template`, `render_template_string` and the stream variants call `_render()` or `_stream()` in `templating.py`. These call `App.fill_template_scope()` (`src/tessel/app.py:588`), which runs every registered injector and merges the results into the context. Values you pass to `render_template` override injector values.\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n- **App-wide:** decorate a function that returns a dict with `@app.ctx_injector`. It is defined as `Scaffold.ctx_injector` in `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:550`.\n- **Bundle-only vs. app-wide from a bundle:** `@bundle.ctx_injector` is meant for that bundle's templates. `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:644`) applies to every template.\n\n  ```python\n  @app.ctx_injector\n  def inject_user():\n      return {\"current_user\": g.get(\"user\")}\n  ```\n- **Static values or functions:** `src/tessel/sansio/app.py:825` assigns to `jinja_env.globals[name or f.__name__]`, so it looks like a global-registration decorator. I didn't read that method's name.\n\nThe `scaffold.py:563` implementation appends to the `None` key, which is the app-wide list. I didn't check whether `Bundle` overrides it, so `@bundle.ctx_injector` may not actually be bundle-scoped despite its docstring.",
